Up until that afternoon, I had never even seen a trophy up close before. Knowing that I had one in between the palms of my slightly sweaty hands—I blame the post-contest, pre-award-ceremony nerves—made me feel like I had accomplished something downright impossible.

Let me take you back a couple of weeks.

![Semifinals Pic](/assets/img/IYF English Speech Comp/Semifinals_Picture.jpg)
![Semifinals Certificate](/assets/img/IYF English Speech Comp/Semifinals_Certificate.jpg)

After receiving the Grand Prize award during the Regional Semifinals and advancing to the National Finals, I felt that something was off. Only when I replayed the video of my speech at the Semifinals did I realize that my presentation…wasn’t that great. I didn’t do as well on the stage as I did during rehearsals. I constantly swayed my body from side to side; I presented with a lack of variety/fluctuation in tone; and it felt obvious that I had memorized the speech beforehand. These were the areas I needed to improve on before the National Finals. If I hadn’t practiced these, let’s face it, my speech would have remained incredibly boring to listen to.

Every day, I would practice these areas as well as some others that I discovered along the way, such as the occasional movement of my eyes that resembled eye-rolling, the frequent blinking of said eyes, and the constant bobbing/shaking of my head. I wasn’t certain I could fix these habits without straining my eyes, seeming unnaturally still, or looking like I was staring into the souls of the audience, but I certainly tried.

One thing that I’ve come to realize about myself is that I’m a better presenter when there is less pressure to do well. For instance, when I present a speech in my academy, since all my classmates are my friends, I remain professional but also joke around more comfortably. I can also talk to the audience in a conversational manner while appearing more confident, and overall, I become a better public speaker in those settings. However, I don’t think I do as well in speech contests due to the added pressure. So, on the day of the competition, I tried my best to think of it as a fun opportunity in a low-pressure environment. (Emphasis on *tried* 🙂)

After finding out that there would be no break in between the 30 middle school finalists’ speeches, I was relieved to have gone as the 10th speaker as I only had to wait 27 minutes until it was my turn to present.

I didn’t just want to prove that I was good at English. I wanted to show them that I was a capable public speaker.

Once my speech was over and the middle school as well as the high school divisions finished presenting all their speeches, it was finally time for the eagerly awaited award ceremony. Every time the emcee was about to present the winners of each prize, I mentally repeated the words “Number 10 Yewon Choi”, hoping I’d be the one called on. After the second place winner reached the stage to accept her prize, I began wondering if I would go home without a prize. But then, my name was called out. First Place. Honestly, I didn’t think I did very well during my presentation, so First Place came as a shock.

After climbing onto the stage and receiving a board that said 700,000 won, a certificate, and a cool trophy, I awkwardly hefted everything onto the large board and into my arms (in front of 100 people). I also ran towards my seat (which was a fairly long distance away from the stage) before being told by the staff that I had to run back near the stage for the group photo. While getting on stage again for the photo, I had to hold everything up with my two hands, which was much more challenging than one would think. I kept worrying that the trophy would slip from my hands and fall onto the person in front of me. Thankfully, me and that person made it out alive safely off the stage. 🙂

